<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<div class="callout" id="at-a-glance">
        <h2>Dead Hang Safety at a Glance</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Stop and step down</strong> for sharp or joint pain, numbness or tingling, light-headedness or feeling faint, a pinching shoulder, or a grip you cannot control. Forearm burning is normal.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask a clinician first</strong> if you have shoulder hypermobility or instability, an acute shoulder injury, recent surgery, or are pregnant.</li>
          <li><strong>If in doubt, start with holds of 10 seconds or fewer</strong> and build from there.</li>
          <li><strong>Breathe normally and get down under control.</strong> Step down onto a box or lower yourself feet first. Never drop.</li>
          <li><strong>The evidence is limited.</strong> No injury-rate data for dead hangs was found, so this page is general guidance and not a prescription.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h2>Dead Hangs Suit Many People</h2>
      <p>Dead hangs are low impact and suit many people. No injury-rate data comparing dead hangs with other exercises was found, so risk is not zero: shoulder, elbow and finger pain and falls from the bar can happen. You support your own bodyweight through a static hold. No ballistic movement exists. No external load beyond gravity applies. The exercise is isometric which means your joints stay in a fixed position throughout.</p>
      <p>Many healthy adults can try short supported dead hangs. The movement needs no special athletic ability. Grip the bar, hang with straight arms and breathe.</p>
      <p>Feet-supported hangs, band-assisted hangs and shortened holds all reduce load for people who currently lack the grip strength to hold their full bodyweight. Build up gradually with the <a href="/beginner-deadhang/#cant-hang-yet">starting ladder</a>.</p>
      <p>The sections below cover the specific populations and situations that require caution or modification. Read them to know exactly where you stand before starting a <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">dead hang program</a>.</p>

      <h2>Who Should Avoid Dead Hangs</h2>
      <p>A small number of conditions make dead hanging inappropriate without medical clearance. These conditions involve acute structural damage or instability that hanging could worsen.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Acute shoulder injuries:</strong> Rotator cuff tears, labral tears, shoulder dislocations and acute bursitis. Hanging loads the shoulder joint capsule and rotator cuff under full bodyweight. Fresh injuries need time to heal before accepting this load.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-surgical recovery:</strong> Any recent shoulder, elbow, wrist or hand surgery. Return to hanging depends on the procedure. Ask your surgeon when overhead hanging is allowed.</li>
        <li><strong>Severe elbow tendinitis:</strong> Active lateral or medial epicondylitis (tennis elbow or golfer's elbow) with sharp pain during gripping. The sustained grip of a dead hang loads the exact tendons that are inflamed. Treat the tendinitis first then return to hanging.</li>
        <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Joint laxity can increase during pregnancy. Ask your midwife or obstetrician before starting or continuing dead hangs while pregnant.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This list of conditions is not exhaustive. Consult a physical therapist or sports medicine doctor if you have any active musculoskeletal condition and want to start dead hang training. Most conditions allow modified hanging rather than complete avoidance.</p>

      <h2 id="ask-first">Who Should Ask a Clinician First</h2>
      <p>Ask a physical therapist or doctor before hanging if you have shoulder hypermobility or instability. That means a diagnosis of either, a history of shoulder dislocations or partial dislocations, or a shoulder that often feels loose or as if it might slip out.</p>
      <p>Two physiotherapy academics, Charlotte Ganderton and Sarah Warby, wrote about this in <a href="https://theconversation.com/what-are-dead-hangs-what-are-the-shoulder-pain-risks-and-how-do-i-do-them-safely-202346">The Conversation (21 April 2023)</a>. They say dead hangs are not the best exercise for people with shoulder hypermobility and instability, and that people with shoulder hypermobility are more likely to develop painful shoulder instability. They advise starting with ten seconds or fewer.</p>
      <p>For non-traumatic shoulder instability the same article points to the Watson Instability Program, which it says has had good results compared with a general shoulder strength program. It also says building shoulder blade control and strength can help. A physical therapist can set that up for you.</p>
      <p>That article is expert opinion, not a trial of dead hangs, and no trial has tested dead hangs in hypermobile people. If you have been cleared to hang, the <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive vs active hang guide</a> explains why some people keep the shoulder blades engaged instead of hanging fully relaxed.</p>

      <h2>Who Should Modify Dead Hangs</h2>
      <p>Modification means reducing load rather than eliminating the exercise. The people below can still hang but should scale the intensity down from full bodyweight.</p>

      <h3>Overweight Beginners</h3>
      <p>Heavier people carry more load through the grip, shoulders and wrists. Foot support or a loop band takes part of your weight. Thicker bands take more. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable, and build up slowly.</p>

      <h3>Older Adults (65+)</h3>
      <p>Grip strength generally declines with age. Start with both feet on the ground and remove weight gradually by bending your knees. Use short supported holds and add time slowly. No age-specific progression has been tested for dead hangs.</p>

      <h3>Returning from Injury</h3>
      <p>Previous shoulder, elbow or wrist injuries create tissue that is healed but not yet fully loaded. Check with a clinician before returning after a diagnosed injury. Use foot support or a loop band to take part of your weight and record the assist you used. Increase load gradually and stop if pain returns at the injury site. The <a href="/injury-prevention/">injury prevention guide</a> covers return-to-training protocols in detail.</p>

      <h2>Pain vs Discomfort</h2>
      <p>Understanding the difference between training discomfort and injury signals keeps you safe. Dead hangs produce predictable sensations that you need to recognize correctly.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Sensation</th>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
            <th scope="col">Action</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Forearm burning</td>
            <td>Normal fatigue</td>
            <td>Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Shoulder stretch/pull</td>
            <td>Normal stretch</td>
            <td>Continue if pain-free</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hand callus discomfort</td>
            <td>Normal friction</td>
            <td>Use chalk or adjust grip</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sharp shoulder pain</td>
            <td>Warning sign</td>
            <td>Stop immediately</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Elbow joint pain</td>
            <td>Warning sign</td>
            <td>Stop and rest; see a clinician if it persists</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Numbness/tingling in fingers</td>
            <td>Possible nerve irritation</td>
            <td>Release the bar immediately</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Wrist clicking with pain</td>
            <td>Joint issue</td>
            <td>Stop and consult a professional</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>Forearm burning is normal fatigue. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control. Do not hang to failure or drop.</p>
      <p>Joint pain is your stop sign. Pain in the shoulder, elbow or wrist joint (not the muscle) is a reason to stop. Release the bar, rest and reassess. Joint pain that persists after rest needs professional evaluation.</p>

      <h2 id="stop-and-step-down">Stop and Step Down If You Notice</h2>
      <p>Any item below ends the hang. Forearm burning and a tired grip are normal and are not on this list.</p>
      <div class="visual-block">
        <div class="dg-box"><div class="dg-box-head"><span>Stop and step down if</span></div>
<ul class="stop-list">
<li><strong>Sharp or joint pain</strong><span>A sudden or stabbing sensation, or pain in a shoulder, elbow, wrist or finger joint. Clicking or popping with pain counts.</span></li>
<li><strong>Numbness or tingling</strong><span>In your hands, fingers or arms.</span></li>
<li><strong>Light-headedness or feeling faint</strong><span>Dizziness, or a sense that you might pass out.</span></li>
<li><strong>A pinching shoulder</strong><span>A pinching feeling in the shoulder that does not ease when you reset your position.</span></li>
<li><strong>Grip slipping uncontrollably</strong><span>Your fingers open, your thumbs lose the bar or the bar rolls in your palms and you cannot hold on.</span></li>
</ul></div>
      </div>
      <h3>What to do</h3>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li>Step down under control. Do not drop.</li>
        <li>If you feel light-headed or faint, sit down until it passes.</li>
        <li>Do not continue that session.</li>
        <li>Seek medical advice if it recurs or persists.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="visual-block">
        ${demoLoopHtml('step-down', { caption: 'Controlled step-down: feet to the box first, then let go of the bar.' })}
      </div>
      <p>Seek urgent help for chest pain, loss of hand control or a suspected dislocation after a fall.</p>

      <h2 id="faint-or-tingling">If You Feel Faint, Tingling or Sharp Pain</h2>
      <p>Get your feet on the ground or a box straight away. A faint on the bar ends in a fall, so do not wait to see whether it passes.</p>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li>Step down under control and sit or lie down until it passes.</li>
        <li>Breathe normally from the start of a hang. Do not hold your breath.</li>
        <li>Do not continue that session, and do not try to hang again to test it.</li>
        <li>See a clinician if it happens again, or if tingling or pain lasts after you let go.</li>
        <li>Seek urgent help if you lose consciousness, have chest pain or a seizure-like episode, or lose control of a hand.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>People online describe feeling faint or passing out during or after hangs, and some offer explanations. No study of dead hangs was found that shows why this happens, so this page does not offer a cause. The advice is to stop and get it checked. Breathing normally is a general precaution, not a finding from a dead hang study.</p>

      <h2 id="getting-down">How to Get Down Safely</h2>
      <p>Lower yourself feet first and step down. Do not let go from height. Grip failure is not a dismount.</p>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li>Before you let go, find the ground or box with your feet. Keep your hands on the bar until your feet take weight.</li>
        <li>Bend your knees as your feet take your weight, then let go.</li>
        <li>Stand still for a moment. If you feel light-headed, sit down.</li>
        <li>Getting on is part of it too. Step off a box under the bar rather than jumping up to it, as The Conversation article advises.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A drop puts a sudden load through the feet, knees and lower back that a controlled step-down avoids. A pop or crack on the way down is not a goal. If it comes with pain, stop and see a clinician. No study of injuries from dismounting dead hangs was found.</p>

      <h2>Equipment Safety</h2>
      <p>Equipment failure during a dead hang drops you to the ground without warning. Check your setup before every session. A quick inspection helps catch problems before you put your weight on the bar.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Doorway bars:</strong> Confirm the bar is seated firmly on the door frame. Push down on the bar with your hands before committing your bodyweight. Check the rubber contact pads for wear. Replace the bar if the pads are cracked or compressed flat.</li>
        <li><strong>Wall-mounted bars:</strong> Test all mounting bolts by gripping the bar and pulling hard. Bolts must be anchored into studs or concrete, not drywall alone. Inspect monthly for loosening caused by vibration.</li>
        <li><strong>Free-standing rigs:</strong> Confirm all pins and J-cups are locked. Check that the rig sits on a level surface without rocking. Weight the base if the rig shifts during use.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight capacity:</strong> Know the rated capacity of your bar. Add your bodyweight plus any weighted vest or belt load. A common rule of thumb is to stay well under the rated maximum user weight, including any added load.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Read the full <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a> for recommended bars and installation methods. Check the mounting instructions and load rating for your exact bar before every session.</p>

      <h2>Fall Prevention</h2>
      <p>Grip failure ends every dead hang set. Your hands will release the bar at some point. Plan for this moment before it happens.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Surface below:</strong> Hang over a rubber mat, carpet or grass. Never hang over concrete, tile or hardwood with nothing underneath. A mat does not make a bad landing safe.</li>
        <li><strong>Hang height:</strong> Keep your feet 15 to 30 cm (6 to 12 inches) from the ground when starting. This reduces fall distance to a controlled step-down. Raise the height only after you can predict your grip failure point consistently.</li>
        <li><strong>Weighted hangs:</strong> Use a spotter when training with a <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">weighted dead hang</a>. The added mass increases momentum during a fall. A spotter can support you as your grip fails and guide you down safely.</li>
        <li><strong>Step-down vs drop:</strong> Train yourself to step down from the bar rather than dropping. Bend your knees slightly as you land. This habit protects your ankles, knees and lower back from repeated impact stress.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="small-copy">This page is general information. It has not been reviewed by a named physical therapist or doctor and does not replace advice about your own body.</p>

      <h2>Returning After a Break</h2>
      <p>Training breaks happen. Illness, travel, injury or life events interrupt your dead hang routine. Returning at your pre-break level raises the chance of overload because your tissues may have de-adapted.</p>
      <p>A cautious option is to cut your training volume to about half for the first two weeks back, including hang time, sets and complementary exercise load. Tendons may take longer than muscles to readapt.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Example Return Plan (Practical Guidance, Not Trial-Tested)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> 50% of pre-break max hold, 2 sets per session, 3 sessions</li>
          <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> 65% of pre-break max hold, 3 sets per session, 3 sessions</li>
          <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> 80% of pre-break max hold, 3 to 4 sets per session, 3 sessions with a rest day between</li>
          <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Resume normal programming if you stayed pain free</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>Check your <a href="/deadhang-form/">form</a> during the first return session. Breaks cause form regression, especially in shoulder position and breathing rhythm. Record yourself and compare to your pre-break technique.</p>
      <p>Watch for common mistakes more closely during the return period. Fatigue sets in faster after a break. Fatigue degrades form. Stay alert and cut sets short if your technique deteriorates.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Is dead hanging safe for beginners?</h3>
        <p>Many healthy beginners can start with supported hangs. Foot support or a loop band takes part of your weight. A short warm-up helps you feel ready. Stop at sharp joint pain, numbness or tingling.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Can dead hangs hurt your shoulders?</h3>
        <p>Some people find gentle hangs comfortable for shoulder stiffness. No trial shows that dead hangs treat impingement. They can cause harm if you have an acute shoulder injury, recent surgery or an unstable joint. Start with short holds of 5 to 10 seconds and increase gradually. Sharp or stabbing shoulder pain means stop and see a physical therapist.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">
        <h3>How do I know if I should stop a dead hang?</h3>
        <p>Stop and step down if you feel sharp or joint pain, numbness or tingling in your hands or fingers, light-headedness or a feeling of faintness, a pinching shoulder, or your grip slipping and you cannot control it. Muscle fatigue and forearm burning are normal sensations. Step down under control, sit if you feel light-headed and do not continue that session. Seek medical advice if it recurs or persists.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Is dead hanging safe if I am hypermobile?</h3>
        <p>Ask a physical therapist or doctor first. Two physiotherapy academics writing in The Conversation say dead hangs are not the best exercise for people with shoulder hypermobility or instability, because hanging the full bodyweight can stretch loose tissue further. If you and your clinician decide to hang, they suggest ten seconds or fewer at first. No trial has tested dead hangs in hypermobile people.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What should I do if I feel faint while dead hanging?</h3>
        <p>Step down under control, sit or lie down until it passes, and do not continue that session. Breathe normally while you hang and do not hold your breath. See a clinician if it happens again. Seek urgent help if you lose consciousness, have chest pain or a seizure-like episode. No study of dead hangs was found that explains why some people feel faint, so this page does not offer a cause.</p>
      </div>

